g tree that became daf2208, and the artifact names the tree it did not use. Impact: CF-091's acceptance criterion 2 promises 'a later lane can diff it against a fresh run'; a lane pinning the cited tip sees a phantom one-row delta, loses the class the census highlights, and cannot tell which tree produced 384. Separately: the comparison groups only rows whose suffix is one of the nine families and exempts -sync by design (family_key returns the full lane name); 73 of the 866 exported rows end in -sync, 3 have a same-named primary, and the artifact reports no count of rows actually compared versus inspected, so its coverage is not auditable. No live divergence hides behind that exemption today: the one divergent -sync row in the export is already rejected by the per-row root check. This row is a review finding only; CF-091's enforcement claims themselves hold (selftest PASS, and disabling the block kills arms 11 and 21).","acceptance_criteria":"1. A detached worktree at the tip named in docs/audits/cf091-repo-url-census.md reproduces that artifact's totals (866 rows, 384 rejected, 482 accepted, exit 2) and prints at least one 'repo_url diverges from primary' line; today the named tip prints 383 rejected / 483 accepted and zero such lines. Name the tree that actually produced the figures (daf2208 or a later commit carrying the check) with a provenance note in docs/SCHEDULER-ADMISSION.md, or re-measure at the named tip once the check is there. CF-091's recorded tree_fp is sha256 of that census file, so a fix that edits the file must also supersede CF-091's evidence entry instead of leaving its digest stale. 2. The census states how many rows the family comparison compared versus how many it inspected (today it reports only rows inspected), so the waiver's coverage is auditable, and the number comes from the tool rather than from prose. 3. bash tests/selftest.sh prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tip."}
